   "evidence": "Line 85 `derivativeSign_C (a x : R) (k : \u2115) : derivativeSign (C a) x k = if k = 0 then sign a else 0`; line 136 `derivativeSign_const (p : R[X]) (a b : R) {k : \u2115} (hk : p.natDegree \u2264 k)`. Grep over `mathlib/Mathlib` for `theorem [a-zA-Z]+_const` returns only \"at a constant\" readings (e.g. `Mathlib/Topology/Basic.lean:119 isOpen_const`, `Mathlib/Computability/PartrecCode.lean:494 eval_const`); grep for `_const` in `mathlib/Mathlib/Algebra/Polynomial` returns no theorem names at all, so there is no polynomial-local precedent for the constancy reading.",
   "file": "TauCeti/Algebra/Polynomial/Thom.lean",
   "fix": "Rename to `derivativeSign_eq_of_natDegree_le`, naming the equality and its hypothesis; this matches Mathlib's `_of_natDegree_le` pattern (`degree_le_of_natDegree_le`, `eq_C_of_natDegree_le_zero`) and is four components, so it does not conflict with the short-name convention invoked earlier in this thread. Update the two uses at lines 150 and 396, 400.",
   "issue": "`derivativeSign_const` states `derivativeSign p a k = derivativeSign p b k` for `p.natDegree \u2264 k`, i.e. independence of the point. In Mathlib the `foo_const` suffix uniformly means \"foo at a constant argument\" (`isOpen_const`, `eval_const`, `mfderiv_const`, `stoppedValue_const`), which here is precisely the neighbouring `derivativeSign_C` at line 85 (`derivativeSign (C a) x k = \u2026`). Two adjacent lemmas where one name plausibly describes the other's statement.",
   "line": "136"
  }
 ],
